A cast to a narrower integer truncates.

micro-c treated a cast as a statement about TYPE alone: primary_expr set
current_target to the cast type and emitted nothing at all. While `int` was
eight bytes that was correct by accident -- every integer cast was
register-width to register-width, so there was nothing to discard.
EXPERIMENT-zzw made `int` four bytes and the accident ended.

Measured, not argued:

    long ci = -32;
    unsigned long v = (unsigned int)ci;
    gcc      v == 0x00000000FFFFFFE0     truncated
    micro-c  v == 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE0     the cast did nothing

THIS IS THE FAULT BEHIND THE FOUR FAILING PROGRAMS IN
stage3-hermetic-arm64. tcc sign-extends a 32-bit local offset by hand, at
arm64-gen.c:494, in load():

    uint64_t svcul = (uint32_t)sv->c.i;
    svcul = svcul >> 31 & 1 ? svcul - ((uint64_t)1 << 32) : svcul;

With the cast a no-op, svcul keeps all 64 bits of a negative offset, the sign
test still fires, and 2^32 is subtracted from a value that was never
truncated. Every local offset comes out 2^32 too small.

That is survivable in one path and fatal in the other, which is exactly why
the failures looked arbitrary:

    svr == (VT_LOCAL | VT_LVAL)   a scalar load, reaches arm64_ldrx, which
                                  emits ldur with `(off & 511)` -- the high
                                  bits are MASKED OFF and the damage never
                                  lands
    svr == VT_LOCAL               the ADDRESS of a local, arm64-gen.c:572,
                                  puts the value into a comparison and then
                                  straight into an instruction word with no
                                  mask at all

So programs using only scalar locals worked, and every program that took the
address of, or indexed, a LOCAL AGGREGATE segfaulted. Globals were unaffected
-- they go through VT_CONST and a symbol. That is the whole of the 05/06/11/12
pattern, and none of it was visible from the compiler's own test suite,
because micro-c compiles those four programs correctly. It was tcc, as micro-c
had built it, that was wrong.

A probe of the idiom alone -- six sub-steps, one bit each -- named the cast in
one run and cost no tcc build:

    bit 1 cast truncates      FAIL      bit 8  sign extension    FAIL
    bit 2 (v >> 31) & 1       ok        bit 16 -svcul == 32      FAIL
    bit 4 (u64)1 << 32        ok        bit 32 range test        FAIL

Bits 2 and 4 passing is what makes this worth writing down: zzb and zze had
already made the shift and the precedence right, so five of the six sub-steps
either passed or failed as a CONSEQUENCE, and only the first was a cause.

ONLY INTEGERS ARE TRUNCATED. A pointer is register-width and needs nothing; a
struct is not held in a register at all; a function pointer is an address.
Each is excluded BY NAME rather than left to fall out of the size test,
because a four-byte struct would otherwise be truncated as though it were an
int -- the same shape of accident this patch is fixing.

ONE SITE, NOT ALL THREE. primary_expr has three places a cast type is applied;
this changes the one that a cast over a postfix expression reaches, which is
where `(uint32_t)sv->c.i` goes. The other two serve `*(T*)x` and deferred
casts through a chain, where the cast type is a pointer in every use in tcc
and truncation would be a no-op. They are left alone deliberately: an untested
emission site is a liability, and cases 101 and 102 cannot reach them.

STAGE-2 SUBSET. No `#define` -- stage 2 discards a `#`-line entire and leaves
the name for stage 1 to fail on, so micro-c's own source expresses every
constant as an enum. AARCH64, AMD64 and TO_FUNCTION_POINTER are enum constants
already (cc.h:37, 39, 121); this patch adds no new constant of any kind.

Requires m2libc patch 0010 for the six aarch64 and five amd64 macros.
Cases 101 and 102. difftest aarch64 84/1 -> 85/0, amd64 82/1 -> 83/0.
